WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.925
David: Welcome to LitShare.

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:05.250
This is Protein Degradation English
Corner.

3
00:00:05.312 --> 00:00:06.562
Episode seven.

4
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.025
Protein Science, Ronald T. Raines.

5
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.550
A Designer KRAS Binder Delivers
Degradation from Outside.

6
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:25.000
This one is about a famously undruggable
protein, and a degrader that comes from
outside the cell.

7
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:27.075
Here is the question.

8
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.750
KRAS drives many cancers, but it has no
pocket for a small molecule drug.

9
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.525
How do you destroy it anyway?

10
00:00:38.775 --> 00:00:42.175
Alex: That is the question a team at M-I-T
set out to answer.

11
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.350
The paper is in Protein Science, 2026.

12
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.875
First authors Aniekan Okon and Keith
Cheah.

13
00:00:51.025 --> 00:00:53.925
Corresponding authors Ronald Raines and
Dane Wittrup.

14
00:00:55.225 --> 00:01:04.775
Their answer is a chimeric degrader: a
designer protein that grabs KRAS,
chemically linked to a small molecule that
recruits an E3 ligase.

15
00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:07.475
David: Start with the problem.

16
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.775
Why is KRAS so hard to drug?

17
00:01:12.375 --> 00:01:17.600
Alex: KRAS is a small G-T-Pase, a
molecular switch that tells cells to grow.

18
00:01:18.275 --> 00:01:20.750
When it mutates, the switch sticks on.

19
00:01:21.575 --> 00:01:27.525
One common mutation is G twelve D, glycine
to aspartate at position twelve.

20
00:01:29.175 --> 00:01:35.250
The problem is the surface: smooth, with
no deep pocket for a small molecule to
grab.

21
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:38.850
David: And targeted protein degradation
should help, right?

22
00:01:39.825 --> 00:01:42.750
You do not need a deep pocket, just a
binder.

23
00:01:43.375 --> 00:01:44.850
Alex: In principle, yes.

24
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:58.675
Targeted protein degradation, T-P-D,
brings an E3 ubiquitin ligase close to the
target, so the ligase tags it with
ubiquitin, and the proteasome destroys it.

25
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:04.250
A small molecule PROTAC does this with two
small-molecule ends.

26
00:02:04.975 --> 00:02:12.675
But for KRAS, even a PROTAC needs a small
molecule that binds KRAS, and that is the
hard part.

27
00:02:13.325 --> 00:02:15.900
David: What about protein-based degraders,
the bioPROTACs?

28
00:02:17.525 --> 00:02:18.725
Alex: Those exist.

29
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:23.575
You fuse a target-binding protein to an
E3-recruiting piece.

30
00:02:24.125 --> 00:02:25.400
The trouble is delivery.

31
00:02:26.275 --> 00:02:38.100
A bioPROTAC is a gene product, expressed
inside the target cell from D-N-A. You
cannot just add it from the outside, the
way you can with a small molecule drug.

32
00:02:38.475 --> 00:02:39.900
David: So this paper bridges the two.

33
00:02:41.425 --> 00:02:42.150
Alex: Exactly.

34
00:02:42.975 --> 00:02:51.750
The team built a chimera: a designer
protein binder, joined by a chemical bond
to a small-molecule V-H-L ligand.

35
00:02:52.225 --> 00:03:01.225
The protein gives the binding, the small
molecule recruits the E3 ligase, and the
whole thing is delivered from outside the
cell.

36
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.775
David: Tell me about the binder.

37
00:03:04.425 --> 00:03:04.975
R eleven.

38
00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:17.750
Alex: R eleven is a small protein, seven
kilodaltons, built on a scaffold called
S-S-O seven D, a heat-stable protein from
a heat-loving microbe.

39
00:03:18.275 --> 00:03:28.850
It binds KRAS G twelve D with an affinity
of four nanomolar, preferring the mutant
over the wild-type protein by about ten-
fold.

40
00:03:29.575 --> 00:03:35.775
It has no cysteines, which matters: the
team wanted to add one cysteine as a
precise attachment point.

41
00:03:35.837 --> 00:03:38.025
David: Why does the attachment point
matter?

42
00:03:40.075 --> 00:03:47.075
Alex: Because they need to link the V-H-L
ligand at exactly one place, without
breaking the part that touches KRAS.

43
00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:49.950
That is engineering problem number one.

44
00:03:51.425 --> 00:03:52.725
They tried two spots.

45
00:03:53.975 --> 00:04:00.400
A cysteine at the N-terminus gave poor
yield: half a milligram per liter, only
eighty percent pure.

46
00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:12.475
A cysteine at the C-terminus, replacing
the last arginine, gave seven times more,
three point five milligrams per liter,
over ninety-five percent pure.

47
00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:16.550
David: So the C-terminal version wins.

48
00:04:17.125 --> 00:04:18.300
Alex: For production, yes.

49
00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:19.925
But there is a cost.

50
00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:31.275
The C-terminal version, once linked to the
V-H-L ligand, binds KRAS G twelve D about
ten times more weakly than the parent
binder.

51
00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:36.775
Still, the team chose it, because the
yield advantage was so large.

52
00:04:37.225 --> 00:04:40.125
David: Engineering problem number two is
delivery, right?

53
00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:42.700
Getting a protein into a cell.

54
00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:43.575
Alex: Right.

55
00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.275
Proteins do not cross cell membranes on
their own.

56
00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:53.475
The team used esterification, with a
reagent called alpha-aryl-alpha-
diazoamide.

57
00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:01.650
It masks the protein's negative charges
with ester groups, so the protein slips
through the membrane.

58
00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:08.800
Inside, the cell's own esterases cut the
esters off, and the protein is free again.

59
00:05:09.075 --> 00:05:10.225
David: Did it work?

60
00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:13.400
Alex: They tested it with a fluorescent
tag, BODIPY.

61
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:21.500
The esterified protein entered C-H-O-K-one
cells, and they could see the glow inside.

62
00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:34.850
Interestingly, R eleven light entered S-W
forty-eight cells even without
esterification, probably because its
arginine-rich surface helps it cross the
membrane on its own.

63
00:05:35.525 --> 00:05:39.450
David: And problem number three: the
binder itself getting ubiquitinated.

64
00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:41.725
Alex: Yes.

65
00:05:42.125 --> 00:05:45.550
The E3 ligase cannot tell the target from
the binder.

66
00:05:46.375 --> 00:05:51.325
R eleven has ten lysines, and lysine is
where ubiquitin attaches.

67
00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:56.025
If the ligase tags the binder, the binder
gets destroyed too.

68
00:05:56.750 --> 00:06:14.100
Three of those lysines, lysine thirty-two,
lysine forty, and lysine fifty-nine, sit
right at the interface with KRAS,
according to a crystal structure, P-D-B
entry five U-F-E. So the team could not
remove those three.

69
00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:22.175
They replaced lysines with arginines,
which keep the positive charge but cannot
be ubiquitinated.

70
00:06:22.725 --> 00:06:23.750
David: Two versions, right?

71
00:06:24.675 --> 00:06:26.600
Lysine-free and lysine-light.

72
00:06:27.425 --> 00:06:28.175
Alex: Yes.

73
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:32.900
R eleven lysine-free has all ten lysines
replaced.

74
00:06:33.575 --> 00:06:39.900
It still binds KRAS, wild type and G
twelve D, with nanomolar affinity.

75
00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:45.075
But when they tried to link it to the
V-H-L ligand, it precipitated.

76
00:06:45.675 --> 00:06:47.275
It was not soluble enough.

77
00:06:48.175 --> 00:06:53.375
R eleven light keeps only the three
interface lysines and replaces the other
seven.

78
00:06:54.625 --> 00:06:59.650
It also keeps nanomolar affinity, and it
conjugates cleanly.

79
00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.350
Their isoelectric points rose to ten point
one nine and nine point six nine, from
nine point two six.

80
00:07:08.725 --> 00:07:10.500
David: Now the key experiment.

81
00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.775
Does it degrade KRAS in cells?

82
00:07:14.425 --> 00:07:36.400
Alex: They tested it in H-P-A-F two cells,
a pancreatic cancer line carrying KRAS G
twelve D. The esterified R eleven V-H-L
conjugate, added from outside, lowered
total pan-RAS levels, all RAS family
proteins together, in a dose-dependent
way.

83
00:07:40.175 --> 00:07:47.600
At ten micromolar, about forty percent was
gone after twenty-four hours, four hours
in serum-free medium, then twenty hours in
complete medium.

84
00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:49.100
David: Forty percent.

85
00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:53.275
But what about KRAS G twelve D itself?

86
00:07:54.175 --> 00:07:55.550
Alex: That is the interesting part.

87
00:07:57.725 --> 00:08:01.875
KRAS G twelve D itself only trended
downward, not statistically significant.

88
00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:17.075
The pan-RAS signal dropped more, probably
because these cells carry far more non-
mutant RAS, about one point three
micromolar, than KRAS G twelve D, and R
eleven also binds wild-type KRAS.

89
00:08:17.825 --> 00:08:21.550
David: How do they know the effect is
really through V-H-L?

90
00:08:22.075 --> 00:08:23.625
Alex: A competition experiment.

91
00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:35.375
Adding a large excess of free V-H-L
ligand, two hundred micromolar, together
with the conjugate, erased the pan-RAS
reduction completely.

92
00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:45.100
So the effect depends on V-H-L. And the
conjugate without esterification did
nothing, so it has to get inside the cell.

93
00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:47.225
David: What about other cell lines?

94
00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:49.750
Alex: That is where it gets complicated.

95
00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:59.900
In two other KRAS G twelve D lines, A-S-
P-C one and S-W forty-eight G twelve D,
the conjugate did nothing.

96
00:09:02.275 --> 00:09:06.325
KRAS G twelve D even went slightly up,
possibly a compensatory response.

97
00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:16.825
The authors do not know why, maybe
differences in uptake, esterase activity,
V-H-L levels, or RAS isoform composition.

98
00:09:16.825 --> 00:09:19.225
David: And the lysine-light version?

99
00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:20.800
Alex: Also mixed.

100
00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:27.875
R eleven light V-H-L worked once in H-P-
A-F two cells, then failed in a repeat.

101
00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:31.250
Removing lysines alone is not enough.

102
00:09:31.875 --> 00:09:35.925
The attachment site, the linker, or the
delivery still need work.

103
00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:37.425
David: So what is the takeaway?

104
00:09:38.575 --> 00:09:39.000
Alex: Two things.

105
00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:49.425
First, a proof of concept: a chimeric
degrader, part protein and part small
molecule, can be delivered from outside
the cell and degrade its target.

106
00:09:50.125 --> 00:09:59.150
Second, design rules: where to put the
conjugation site, how to protect the
binder from self-destruction, and how to
get it inside.

107
00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:10.100
The approach fits targets that already
have a protein binder but no small-
molecule ligand, a fast way to test
whether degrading that target is worth
pursuing.

108
00:10:10.825 --> 00:10:12.825
David: A modest result, but an honest one.

109
00:10:14.375 --> 00:10:14.725
Alex: Exactly.

110
00:10:15.575 --> 00:10:21.750
Ten micromolar, about forty percent
degradation, only in one of three cell
lines.

111
00:10:22.375 --> 00:10:23.375
It is not a drug.

112
00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:28.725
It is a tool, and a set of design
principles for building the next version.

113
00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:31.075
David: That is it for this episode.

114
00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:35.850
You have been listening to Protein
Degradation English Corner, from Litshare.

115
00:10:36.375 --> 00:10:37.325
See you next time.
